<BlogArticle
	title="How to Resize a PAN Card Photo to 213 × 213 Pixels"
	description="Learn how to create a square 213 × 213 PAN photo and why this legacy UTIITSL preset must be checked against the current Form 93/94 instructions."
	canonicalUrl="https://nsdlphotoresize.com/blogs/uti-pan-photo-213x213/"
	heading="How to resize a PAN card photo to 213 × 213 pixels"
	intro="The 213 × 213 pixel square is a familiar UTIITSL photo preset. However, the current official portals now direct Indian new-PAN applicants to revised Forms 93/94, so verify the live form before treating that older number as the current specification."
>
	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Is 213 × 213 still the current UTIITSL specification?</h2>
		<p>
			UTIITSL’s public PAN page states that new Indian applications
			should use Forms 93 and 94 from 1 April 2026 and that old Forms
			49A/49AA are no longer accepted after 31 March 2026. Protean’s
			current application guidance also points Indian applicants to
			Forms 93/94. The public current-form guidance does not establish
			213 × 213 pixels as a universal requirement for every new
			application.
		</p>
		<p>
			Therefore, treat 213 × 213 as a legacy UTIITSL preset unless the
			current application flow you are using explicitly requests those
			dimensions. The form’s own upload field and instructions take
			priority over an old “UTI photo resize” article, tool, or search
			result. We checked the public Form 93/94 guidance on 29 October
			2026; check it again when applying because portal rules can change.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">If the current form asks for 213 × 213</h2>
		<ol class="list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6">
			<li>Start with a clear, recent color photo that meets the form’s pose and background rules.</li>
			<li>Open the <a class="text-link" href="/#photo-tool">PAN card photo resizer</a> and choose the photo from your device.</li>
			<li>Set both width and height to 213 pixels, then position the square crop so the face is centered and not clipped.</li>
			<li>Export in the file format and size limit shown by the current application. Do not assume an older 30 KB cap or 300 DPI value still applies.</li>
			<li>Check the saved image properties: both pixel dimensions should read 213, and the file should open clearly.</li>
		</ol>
	</section>

	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Keep the photo square without distorting it</h2>
		<p>
			Use a square crop rather than stretching a portrait photo until
			it becomes square. Cropping changes the visible edges; stretching
			changes the shape of the face. Keep the subject at the scale
			requested by the application, and retain enough margin around the
			head. A square shape alone does not guarantee a valid PAN photo.
		</p>
	</section>

	<p>
		Review the <a class="text-link" href="https://www.pan.utiitsl.com/PAN/newA.html">official UTIITSL Form 93/94 information</a>
		and <a class="text-link" href="https://tinpan.proteantech.in/services/pan/instructions-form_93-form_94.html">Protean’s Form 93/94 instructions</a>
		before uploading. This site is an independent image tool and is not
		affiliated with UTIITSL or Protean.
	</p>
</BlogArticle>
